                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#for_fundamentally_nonvanishing_cc"} For fundamentally non-vanishing cc
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In theories of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmology"} cosmology
            a
            %em cosmological constant
            refers to an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy"} energy
            contribution associated with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
            itself.
          %p
            This is called a
            %em cosmological constant
            because the simplest way to see this effect in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theory
            is by a summand to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Hilbert+action"} Einstein-Hilbert action
            which is proportional to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/volume"} volume
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:EinsteinHilbertWithCosmologicalConstant"} (1)
            below.
          %p See below
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#InClassicalGravity"} In classical gravity
          %p More generally, almost-constant contributions to matter fields may effectively have the same kind of effect.
          %p
            Specifically in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT+on+curved+spacetimes"} on curved spacetimes
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+expectation+value"} vacuum expectation value
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stress-energy+tensor"} stress-energy tensor
            of the various
            = succeed "-fields" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matter"} matter
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            freedom (e.g.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Hack15"} Hack 15, section 3.2.1
          %p See below
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#InPerturbativeQuantumGravity"} In perturbative quantum gravity
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory"} perturbative string theory
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+perturbation+series"} string perturbation series
            associated with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+SCFT"} 2d SCFT
            is (supposedly) UV-finite and hence has “chosen its own
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory+vacuum"} perturbative string theory vacuum
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#ReferencesInStringTheory"} In string theory
          %p
            Observation shows that the effective cosmological constant of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} observable universe
            is comparatively small but
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+real+number"} positive
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a{:href => "#Observation"} Observation
              \.
          %h2#details Details
          %h3#InClassicalGravity In classical gravity
          %p
            In an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            on a space of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+manifolds"} pseudo-Riemannian manifolds
            – such as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Hilbert+action"} Einstein-Hilbert action
            functional for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            – a
            %strong cosmological constant
            is a term proportional to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/volume"} volume
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi cc
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi λ
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mi dvol
                    %mi g
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S_{cc} \;\colon\;  (X,g) \mapsto \lambda \int_X dvol_g
                  \\,,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda \in \mathbb{R}
            is the
            %em cosmological constant
            \.
          %p For instance, pure Einstein-Hilbert gravity with cosmological constant (and no other fields) is given by the functional
          #eq:EinsteinHilbertWithCosmologicalConstant.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi EH
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi cc
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi X
                  %mi R
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi dvol
                  %mo +
                  %mi λ
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi X
                  %mi d
                  %msub
                    %mi vol
                    %mi g
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S_{EH} + S_{cc} :  (X,g) \mapsto \int_X R\, dvol + \lambda \int_X d vol_g
                  \\,,
          %p
            Generically it happens that one considers action functionals where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            is in fact not a constant, but a function of other fields
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi X
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi dvol
                    %mi g
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S \;\colon\; (X,g,\phi) \mapsto \int_X \lambda(\phi) dvol_g
                  \\,.
          %p
            In this context those solutions to the
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler-Lagrange+equation"} Euler-Lagrange equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda(\phi)
            happens to be exactly or approximately constant. Many such models of not-really-constant-but-effectively-constant terms proportional to the volume are being proposed and considered in attempts to explain observed or speculated dynamics of the cosmos.
          %p
            See in particular at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FRW+model"} FRW model
            for the role of the cosmological constant in homogeneous and isotropic models as in the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+cosmology"} standard model of cosmology
            %em dark energy
            (density), which makes up about 70% of the energy density of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} observable universe
            (the rest being
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dark+matter"} dark matter
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/baryon"} baryonic
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matter"} matter
          %h3#InPerturbativeQuantumGravity In perturbative quantum gravity
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT+on+curved+spacetime"} on curved spacetimes
            the cosmological constant receives contributions from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+expectation+value"} vacuum expectation value
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stress-energy+tensor"} stress-energy tensor
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matter"} matter
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            = succeed "-freedom" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Wald78"} Wald 78
            %a{:href => "#TichyFlanagan98"} Tichy-Flanagan 98
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Moretti01"} Moretti 01
          %p
            Explicitly for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FRW+models"} FRW models
            this is discussed in (
            %a{:href => "#DappiaggiFredenhagenPinamonti08"}> Dappiaggi-Fredenhagen-Pinamonti 08
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#DappiagiHackMollerPinamonti10"} Dappiagi-Hack-Moeller-Pinamonti 10
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+cosmology"} standard model of cosmology
            see (
            %a{:href => "#Hack13"}> Hack 13, around (4)
            ).
          %p
            A useful review is in (
            %a{:href => "#Hack15"}> Hack 15, section 3.2.1
            ).
          %p
            This means that apart from the freedom of choosing a classical comsological constant in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Hilbert+action"} Einstein-Hilbert action
            as above, its
            %a{:href => "A+first+idea+of+quantum+field+theory#InteractingQuantumFields"} perturbative quantization
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+gravity"}> perturbative quantum gravity
            ) introduces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            freedom to the value of the cosmological constant.
          %p
            The folklore discussion of the “cosmological constant problem” (see the references
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#CCProblemReferences"} below
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/naturalness"} naturalness
          %h3#InInhomogeneousCosmology In inhomogeneous cosmology
          %p
            It has been suggested the observed cosmological constant/dark energy may be but an artifact of the overly idealistic approximation of cosmic homogeneity, and that a more accurate
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhomogeneous+cosmology"} inhomogeneous cosmology
            would not need to assume any dark energy (e.g.
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Buchert07"} Buchert 07
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Buchert11"} Buchert 11
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BucherRasanen11"} Buchert-Rasanen 11
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Scharf13"} Scharf 13
          %p
            A seminal argument that it
            %em is
            consistent to neglect cosmic inhomogeneity due to (
            %a{:href => "#GreenWald10"}> Green-Wald 10
            ,
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#GreenWald13"} Green-Wald 13
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BuchertEtAl15"} Buchert et al. 15
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#BelejkoKorzynski16"} Belejko-Korzyński 16
          %p
            If the apparent small positive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
            were but an artifact of neglecting backreaction of inhomogeneities, some theoretical puzzlement regarding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Sitter+spacetimes"} de Sitter spacetimes
            would disappear (see
            %a{:href => "de+Sitter+spacetime#Rajaraman16"} Rajaraman 16
            for general discussion and
            %a{:href => "#DanielssonVanRiet18"} Danielsson-VanRiet 18, p. 27
            for discussion of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory+vacua"} perturbative string theory vacua
          %h3#Observation Observation
          %p
            %img{:src => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/DarkEnergyConcordance.gif", :width => "500"}/
          %p
            (e.g.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Einasto09"} Einasto 09, fig 17
